Text 2: Cost alone cannot explain the press''s impact. Because it produced identical copies, scholars in distant cities could cite the same page and correct the same errors, building knowledge they shared.', 75, 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the view in Text 1?', '["By agreeing that lower cost was the only important effect of the press.", "By arguing that the press''s influence also depended on producing identical copies that scholars could share.", "By claiming that books became more expensive after the press appeared.", "By denying that the printing press spread new ideas."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Text 2 says cost ''alone'' cannot explain the impact, so it accepts cost as a factor and adds identical copies as another.', false),
  ('r-s07', 'writing', 'rhetorical-synthesis', 4, 'session', 'delegation', 'While researching a topic, a student took these notes:
• The axolotl is a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City.
• It can regrow lost limbs and parts of its heart and brain.
• Scientists study it to learn how tissue regeneration might work in humans.
• Wild populations are critically endangered.', 75, 'The student wants to emphasize why scientists are interested in the axolotl. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The axolotl, a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City, is critically endangered in the wild.", "Because the axolotl can regrow limbs and even parts of its heart and brain, scientists study it to learn how tissue regeneration might work in humans.", "The axolotl is a salamander that lives in lakes.", "Wild axolotls live near Mexico City, where some scientists work."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Start from the goal, not the notes. Only the second choice names the ability and the reason scientists study it.', false),
  ('r-s08', 'writing', 'rhetorical-synthesis', 3, 'session', 'delegation', 'While researching a topic, a student took these notes:
